From: tokenscope — https://github.com/PS12007/tokenscope
Subject: [layout control, do not merge] a 16-byte code shift with no behaviour change

This is not a proposal. It is the layout arm for audit issue M6 — the question
of how much of FINDINGS F24/F33's +1.62% decode improvement is the scheduler
change and how much is code layout — and it exists to be measured and reverted.

Why 16 bytes. F42 read the linker map for F24's two arms and found that their
1,137,994 differing bytes are one thing: `ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat` shrinks
by 16 bytes, and all 7,721 functions after it move down by exactly 16. Every
other function is unmoved. There is no regeneration and no reordering — F38
concluded otherwise from a byte-window probe that cannot detect relocation in
code containing relocated absolute addresses.

So the layout question is not "what does a third of a rewritten image do". It is
"what does a 16-byte shift of the hot code do", and that is reproducible without
touching behaviour.

What this does. Adds a never-called function between `mul_mat` and
`mul_mat_id`, kept alive by a volatile pointer so `/OPT:REF` cannot drop it and
given a unique body so `/OPT:ICF` cannot fold it onto another trivial function.
It occupies 16 bytes of address space and nothing else.

Verified against the map before measuring, which is the step F38's control
skipped:

  - exactly two address deltas across all 14,419 functions in `.text`:
    0 for 6,689 of them and **+16** for 7,730
  - `ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat` is **the same size** in both arms (3,664
    bytes) and 98.6% byte-identical after accounting for its move; the 51 bytes
    that differ are relocated absolute addresses, which must change when their
    targets move
  - `ggml_vec_dot_f32`, the hottest function in an F32 decode, moves +16 and its
    alignment goes from 0 to 16 mod 64 (F24's arm moves it to 48 mod 64)

Known asymmetry, stated because it limits the conclusion. F24's arm shifts
downstream code by **-16** and holds `mul_mat` fixed while changing its contents.
This arm shifts by **+16** and holds `mul_mat`'s contents fixed while moving it.
The perturbation is the same size and affects the same hot function; it is not
the identical perturbation, and an alignment effect need not be symmetric. A
null result here bounds the layout effect for a 16-byte shift on this machine.
It does not prove layout is never worth a percent.

--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
@@ -1459,6 +1459,19 @@ UseGgmlGemm2:;
     }
 }
 
+// [tokenscope layout control -- DO NOT MERGE] Never called. Its only purpose is
+// to occupy address space immediately after ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat, so
+// that every function below it shifts while nothing about the computation
+// changes. FINDINGS F42 showed that F24's patch shifts exactly this region by
+// 16 bytes; this reproduces that shift without the behaviour change.
+// Kept alive by a volatile pointer so /OPT:REF cannot drop it, and given a
+// unique body so /OPT:ICF cannot fold it onto some other trivial function.
+volatile int ggml_ts_layout_sink;
+static void ggml_ts_layout_pad(void) {
+    ggml_ts_layout_sink = 0x5a17c0de;
+}
+void (* volatile ggml_ts_layout_keep)(void) = ggml_ts_layout_pad;
+
 // ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat_id
 
 #define MMID_MATRIX_ROW(row_id, i1) matrix_rows[(row_id)*ids->ne[0]*ids->ne[1] + (i1)]
